{
  "dom_id": "step-q94643",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{6}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =\\sqrt[6]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m\\ge 1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
